        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 876,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures how spread out a set of angles is, around the circle - the circular standard deviation. Near 0 when the angles agree; larger the more they scatter.\n\nnp.std treats angles as ordinary numbers, so a tight bunch either side of 180 (170 and -170, only 20 degrees apart) looks hugely spread. scipy.circstd measures the real scatter of the directions. Use it to tell whether a performer keeps a steady heading or wanders, how consistently a joint returns to the same angle, how locked two rhythms' phases are.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.circstd <axis: int> <units>\nDefaults: axis None (every value together), degrees.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nangles:\nThe angles (a list or array). Every input is measured and sent. nan values are skipped.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone measures every angle together. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: degrees (the default) or radians.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspread:\nThe circular standard deviation, in the units chosen.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n170 and -170, two headings 20 degrees apart: scipy.circstd gives about 10 degrees;\nnp.std gives 170. A metro re-sends the angles twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.circmean gives the average direction. np.std measures the spread of ordinary values.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 860,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures how spread out a set of angles is, around the circle - the circular standard deviation. Near 0 when the angles agree; larger the more they scatter.\n\nnp.std treats angles as ordinary numbers, so a tight bunch either side of 180 (170 and -170, only 20 degrees apart) looks hugely spread. scipy.circstd measures the real scatter of the directions. Use it to tell whether a performer keeps a steady heading or wanders, how consistently a joint returns to the same angle, how locked two rhythms' phases are.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.circstd <axis: int> <units>\nDefaults: axis None (every value together), degrees.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nangles:\nThe angles (a list or array). Every input is measured and sent. nan values are skipped.\n\naxis (text box, also an inlet):\nNone measures every angle together. A number measures along that axis: for a 2-D array, 0 gives one answer per column.\n\nunits:\nAn option, under the node: degrees (the default) or radians.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspread:\nThe circular standard deviation, in the units chosen.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\n170 and -170, two headings 20 degrees apart: scipy.circstd gives about 10 degrees;\nnp.std gives 170. A metro re-sends the angles twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.circmean gives the average direction. np.std measures the spread of ordinary values."
        },
